        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-09-01T09:40:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1123,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 1162,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 156242,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 269,
        "headingId": 1162,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Synovial venous malformations, synovial arteriovenous malformations</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-09-01T09:40:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1124,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 1163,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 156243,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 269,
        "headingId": 1163,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The most common site is the knee, followed by the elbow, wrist, and ankle. Involvement of hip or other joints is exceptionally rare {24061493; 8319952}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-09-01T09:40:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1125,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 1164,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 156244,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 269,
        "headingId": 1164,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most patients with SVAs are symptomatic and frequently present with joint swelling, effusion, persistent pain, and limitation of movement {8319952; 34950698}. Recurrent haemarthrosis has been reported {17588975; 21088622}. Delayed diagnosis may cause osteoarthritic damage. Rarely, destructive growth may be seen {20385009}, exceptionally with extra-articular extension, bone involvement, and pathological fracture of bone {21773788; 18758797}. Synovial lesions are frequently associated with adjacent cutaneous or deep soft tissue vascular anomalies. MRI is the best radiological technique for diagnosis and for determining the extent of involvement {8614857}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-09-01T09:40:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1126,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 1165,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 156245,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 269,
        "headingId": 1165,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>SVAs are extremely rare lesions, predominantly affecting children and adolescents, with no significant sex predilection {8319952; 30980138}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-09-01T09:40:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1127,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 1166,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 156246,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 269,
        "headingId": 1166,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-09-01T09:40:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1128,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 1167,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 156247,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 269,
        "headingId": 1167,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>It is probable that these lesions are driven by the same somatic mutations identified in their counterparts at other anatomical sites.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-09-01T09:40:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1135,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 1174,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 156248,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 269,
        "headingId": 1174,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>SVAs may be either well circumscribed or diffuse. Dilated vascular structures, sometimes containing thrombi and phleboliths, are commonly observed. Some lesions may display a more solid and even nodular architecture. The overlying villous synovium often shows a characteristic mahogany-brown discolouration {8319952}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-09-01T09:40:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1129,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 1168,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 156249,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 269,
        "headingId": 1168,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The lesions display variable histological features depending on the subtype. Venous malformations are composed of spongiform, variably sized, dysmorphic venous channels, often associated with thrombosis and phleboliths {12447041}.</p>\r\n<p>Arteriovenous malformations are infiltrative lesions comprising small, medium, and large arteries with loss of the internal elastic lamina, and veins of similar calibre exhibiting thickened walls and intimal hyperplasia. A diffuse or lobular proliferation of small vessels with plump endothelial cells and pericytes may also be observed {21095470}.</p>\r\n<p>Villous hyperplasia of the synovium is present in some cases, particularly in chronic lesions with recurrent haemarthrosis, and may show prominent iron deposition {8319952}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-09-01T09:40:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1130,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 1169,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 156250,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 269,
        "headingId": 1169,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Insufficient data</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-09-01T09:40:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1131,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 1170,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 156251,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 269,
        "headingId": 1170,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-09-01T09:40:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1134,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 1173,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 156252,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 269,
        "headingId": 1173,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> benign vascular anomalies arising in synovium-lined spaces morphologically within the spectrum of venous or arteriovenous malformation.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> compatible imaging.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-09-01T09:40:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1132,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 1171,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 156253,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 269,
        "headingId": 1171,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not applicable</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-09-01T09:40:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1133,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 1172,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 156254,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 269,
        "headingId": 1172,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>SVAs are benign lesions that can be managed surgically. Small, well-circumscribed lesions are usually amenable to complete excision via synovectomy, with minimal risk of local recurrence {30980138}. In contrast, when the lesion shows more diffuse intra-articular involvement, complete resection may be challenging. There are no published studies addressing recurrence risk or prognosis stratified by specific histological subtypes.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2026-09-01T09:40:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]